# GC197: Painless jaundice and epigastric mass, pancreatic and CBD cancers

- **DDx of malignant biliary obstruction:**
  - <u>Peri-ductal tumours</u> - CA Head of Pancreas, Extrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma (distal or hilar), CA ampulla of Vater, CA duodenum
  - <u>LN metastasis to porta-hepatis</u> - CA stomach, gallbladder, HCC, CA Pancreas (body or tail), lymphoma
- **Clinical presentation of MBO:**
  - <u>Progressive painles jaundice</u> - tea-coloured urine, pale-coloured stools and generalised pruritis that is often subacute/ chronic, non-remitting and progressive
  - <u>Epigastric mass</u> - ocassionally due to local mass effect of the tumour or hepatomegaly due to biliary obstruction
- **Signs of MBO:**
  - <u>Courvoisier sign</u> - palpable gallbladder in patient presenting w/ painless jaundice unlikely to be caused by stone disease as chronic cholecystitis will cause fibrosis of the GB and therefore impalpable, with exceptions of:
    - **Double stone** - cystic duct and CBD
    - Recurrent pyogenic cholangitis
    - Mirizzi syndrome
- **Ix:**
  - **Routine bloods** - CBC, LRFT\< Clotting, Amylase, Blood C/ST, tumour markers:
  - **Imaging** - abdominal USG and CT
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>Treat biliary sepsis</u> - biliary drainage if septic
    - <u>Diagnosis and staging evaluation</u> - accurate assessment and staging of the inciting malignancy and determination of resectability (patient factor, tumour factor)
    - <u>Appropriate Mx for tumour</u>:
      - Resectable disease - Whipple's operation
      - Unressectable disease - PTBD, ERCP w/ stenting or bypass surgery
  - **Evaluation of MBO:**
    - <u>Abdominal USG</u> - demonstration of biliary obstruction by presence dilated biliary system
    - <u>CT abdomen</u> - tri-phasic CT scan on pancreatic protocol (diagnostic)
    - \+*- <u>Direct cholangiography</u> - ERCP or PTC* MRCP if ERCP contraindicated
    - <u>PET-CT</u> - staging of malignancy
    - <u>Endoscopic USG</u> - for locoregional staging +/- Bx
    - <u>Diagnostic laparotomy</u> - r/o peritoneal metastasis prior to radical resection
  - **Non-surgical palliative options for MBO** - for patients w/ definite unresectable tumour found by preoperative imagings:
    - **Selection** - ERCP w/ stenting (endoprosthesis) vs percutaneous transhepatic biliary drainage (PTBD)
    - **Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography** (ERCP) - preferred over PTBD:
      - <u>Indications</u> - palliative drainage or pre-operative drainage for Whipple's Condition
      - <u>C/I</u>:
        - Anaesthetic risk - unable to tolerate GA or MAC
        - Haemodynamic instability
        - Altered GI anatomy - reconstruction from prior gastrectomy (e.g. B2) or as a result of GOO
      - <u>Stent selection</u> - self-expandable metalic stent (SEMS) vs plastic stent
    - **Percutaneous transhepatic biliary drainage** (PTBD)

## Malignant biliary obstruction

- **Definition** - Obstruction of the biliary tract by cancer growth within or around the biliary tract
- **Jaundice** - medical vs surgical:
  - Medical - pre-hepatic (haemolysis), intrahepatic (hepatitis, cirrhosis)
  - Surgical jaundice:
    - CBD stone
    - Benign stricture of bile ducts (rare) - RPC, PSC, TB
    - Tumours (MBO):
      - Malignant - Cholangiocarcinoma, CA pancreas, Peri-ampullary carcinoma, CA duodenum, LN over porta-hepatis (e.g. Lymphoma, CA stomach), CA gallbladder (direct compression), HCC
- DDx of obstructive jaundice
  - 
- **Etiology of MBO:**
  - Extrahepatic compression by solid organ tumour:
    - CA pancreas (head)
    - Extra-hepatic Cholangiocarcinoma - distal or hilar (Klatskin tumor)
    - CA ampulla of vater
    - CA duodenum (rare)
    - CA gallbladder - via direct infiltration, intraluminal obstruction or cystic duct LN
    - HCC
  - LN in porta-hepatis:
    - Lymphoma
    - LN metastasis fro CA stomach, CA pancreas (body or head), CA gallbladder, HCC
- Salient points of Hx of obstructive jaundice:
  - Onset and progression - Progressive or intermittent
  - Confirm obstructive jaundice - tea-coloured urine (most sensitive Sx, esp in asians), pale stool, generalised pruritis
  - Painful or painless:
    - Painful jaundice - likely CBD stones
    - Painless jaundice - likely MBO
  - Any features of ascending cholangitis - fever/ chills/ rigors
  - GI Sx - change of bowel habits, vomiting
  - Sx of malignancy - weightloss
  - PMH/SH - HBV, HCV, drinking
- Ix:
  - Non-invasive:
    - USG - dilated intra-hepatic biliary ducts (cannot visualise CBD well due to obscured by duodenum)
    - CT - dilated intrahepatic duct (typically run adjacent to portal veins) +/- hilar mass (Klatskin)
      - Double duct sign - dilated CBD and pancreatic duct (ddx Pancreatic head CA, obstruction at Ampulla)
    - MRI and MRCP - confirm biliary obstruction by MRI (non-invasive, superceding ERCP for <u>diagnostic purposes</u>)
    - PET-CT - Dx of cancer, pre-operative staging, and operative assesment
  - Invasive:
    - EUS - identify mass in pancreas, bile duct, and gallbladder, locoregional assessment (vascular, LN), +/- FNA for unresectable cases
    - ERCP - diagnostic and therapeutic, allows biopsy/ brush cytology and biliary stenting:
      - Complications - bleeding (from ampulla vater manipulation), acute pancreatitis, perforation
    - PTC or PTBD

## Surgery for MBO

- role - curative or palliative (relief of obstruction)
- Dependent on general status and tumour status
- Assessment of general status - fitness for GA and comorbidities:
  - Age
  - Comorbidities - DM, AF/ IHD, COPD etc.
  - Hidden medical illness - ECG. blood sugar, RFT
  - Current meds - anti-platelets and anticoagulants
- Tumour staging - unresectable if distant metastasis or vascular involvement (e.g. SMA, coeliac trunk)
  - Clinical signs of inoperativity - Virchow's node, irregular surface hepatomegaly (note smooth hepatomegaly can be atttributed to MBO), Umbilical nodule, malignant ascies, rectal-vesical pouch deposit
  - Radiological signs of inoperativity - distant mets, distant LN involvement, Celiac/SMA encasement, SMV/PV encasement are relative c/i as allows resect and anastamose
  - \+*- laparoscopy* laparotomy - r/o peritoneal spread (e.g. CT shows small peritoneal nodule, but PET is not active)
- Whipple Operation (treatment of curative intent) - resection of duodenum, pancreatic head, CBD, gallbladder and D2 LN dissection + reconstruction (triple-ostomy)
  - Operative mortality - 3-5% reduced due to better understanding of pathophysiological disturbance of MBO, hence better pre-op Bx:
    - Bleeding tendancy - due to vit K deficiency (IV Vit K +/- FFP)
    - Impaired protein synthesis - necessary for wound and anastamosis healing (IV nutritional support)
    - Endotoxemia (due to reduced reticuloendothelial involvement) - due to reduced function of Kupffer cells (IV ABx ABx)
    - Concomittent cholangitis:
      - ?Drainage by PTBD drainage and endoprosthesis –\> but can risk contamination and septic complications –\> so not recommended as it increases risk of post-op if essentially borderline LFT or even normal (may be considered if severe cholangitis picture)
- Palliative Tx:
  - Non surgical bypass - typically definite unresectable tumour from preoperative imaging
  - Surgical bypass - typically performed if only revealed unresectable intraoperatively
- Bypass surgery:
  - Single bypass - bypass biliary tree
  - Double bypass - gastrojejunostomy (typically if CA pancreas cause GOO)
  - Triple bypass - add pancreaticojejunostomy to relieve pain caused by obstructed pancreatic drug
- Non surgical
  - Endoprosthesis - plastic vs metallic (different properties in terms of ease of removal and lifespan)
  - PTBD - typically if endoprosthesis fail, e.g. hilum obstruction
